Here's how you can create an input field with a clickable SVG search icon on the right side that triggers a function when clicked:

```html
<div style="position: relative; display: inline-block;">
  <input
    type="text"
    id="searchInput"
    placeholder="Search..."
    style="padding-right: 30px; /* Make space for the icon */"
  />
  <svg
    xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
    width="16"
    height="16"
    viewBox="0 0 24 24"
    fill="none"
    stroke="currentColor"
    stroke-width="2"
    stroke-linecap="round"
    stroke-linejoin="round"
    style="
      position: absolute;
      right: 8px;
      top: 50%;
      transform: translateY(-50%);
      cursor: pointer;
    "
    onclick="handleSearch()"
  >
    <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
    <path d="m21 21-4.35-4.35"></path>
  </svg>
</div>

<script>
  function handleSearch() {
    const inputValue = document.getElementById('searchInput').value;
    console.log('Searching for:', inputValue);
    // Add your search logic here
    // For example: performSearch(inputValue);
  }
</script>
```

### Key features:
1. The input and icon are wrapped in a `div` with `position: relative`
2. The icon is absolutely positioned on the right side of the input
3. The input has `padding-right` to prevent text from being hidden behind the icon
4. The SVG has `cursor: pointer` to indicate it's clickable
5. The `onclick` handler calls the `handleSearch` function
6. The function gets the input value and can perform your search logic

### Alternative with React (if you're using React):
```jsx
import { useState } from 'react';

function SearchInput() {
  const [searchTerm, setSearchTerm] = useState('');

  const handleSearch = () => {
    console.log('Searching for:', searchTerm);
    // Add your search logic here
  };

  return (
    <div style={{ position: 'relative', display: 'inline-block' }}>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={searchTerm}
        onChange={(e) => setSearchTerm(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Search..."
        style={{ paddingRight: '30px' }}
      />
      <svg
        xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
        width="16"
        height="16"
        viewBox="0 0 24 24"
        fill="none"
        stroke="currentColor"
        strokeWidth="2"
        strokeLinecap="round"
        strokeLinejoin="round"
        style={{
          position: 'absolute',
          right: '8px',
          top: '50%',
          transform: 'translateY(-50%)',
          cursor: 'pointer'
        }}
        onClick={handleSearch}
      >
        <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
        <path d="m21 21-4.35-4.35"></path>
      </svg>
    </div>
  );
}
```

You can customize the styling and icon as needed. The search icon is a simple magnifying glass SVG, but you can replace it with any other SVG icon you prefer.

